(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("db-ops",{"db-ops/postgresql-replication-and-high-availability":{"html":"\u003cp>A single database server is a single point of failure. Replication keeps copies, and a failover process promotes one when the primary dies. The hard part is doing it without losing data or ending up with two primaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Write ahead log and streaming replication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Asynchronous versus synchronous replication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication lag and what it means for reads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automatic failover with Patroni and a consensus store\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Split brain, fencing and promotion safety\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"wal-and-streaming\">WAL and streaming\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>PostgreSQL writes every change to the write ahead log first. A standby connects to the primary and replays that log continuously. The standby is a read only copy that can serve queries and take over. Replication slots keep the log until each standby has received it, but a stuck standby can fill the primary disk with retained log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"asynchronous-or-synchronous\">Asynchronous or synchronous\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In asynchronous replication the primary commits without waiting, so it is fast but a crash can lose the last few transactions. Synchronous replication waits for at least one standby, which avoids loss but adds latency and can block writes if the standby is gone. Quorum settings such as \u003ccode>ANY 1 (s1, s2)\u003c/code> give a compromise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lag\">Lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Replication lag is the delay between the primary and a standby. Measure it in bytes and in seconds. Reads from a lagging standby can return old data, so route read your writes queries to the primary or wait for a log position. Alert on lag before it becomes minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automatic-failover\">Automatic failover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Patroni runs next to each PostgreSQL instance and keeps a leader key in a consensus store such as etcd. If the leader key expires, the healthiest standby is promoted and the others follow it. Applications connect through a proxy or a virtual address that points to the current leader.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"split-brain-and-fencing\">Split brain and fencing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Split brain means two nodes both accept writes. Prevent it with a consensus store, a watchdog that restarts a node that cannot reach it and a proxy that checks the role. After a failover, the old primary must not rejoin as primary: it needs to be rewound or rebuilt from the new one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;pri;pri&gt;stb;stb&gt;dcs\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">PostgreSQL replication and high availability\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pri\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Primary\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stb\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Standby\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dcs\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consensus store\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Primary; 2, Primary to Standby; 3, Standby to Consensus store.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes and reads.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hard coded host.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pri\">\u003cdt>Primary \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accepts writes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Crash.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stb\">\u003cdt>Standby \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Replays WAL.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lag.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dcs\">\u003cdt>Consensus store \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the leader key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Quorum lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-primary-that-filled-its-disk-with-retained-logs\">Incident: A primary that filled its disk with retained logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A forgotten replica made the primary retain WAL until the disk was full.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) as retained from pg_replication_slots&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) as retained from pg_replication_slots&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> slot_name | active | retained\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> old_rep_2 | f      | 412 GB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A standby decommissioned weeks earlier still had a replication slot, so the primary kept every log segment since then. Dropping the slot freed the space, and the team added an alert on retained WAL per slot and a step in the decommission checklist.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgotten replication slots\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Synchronous replication with a single standby\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No fencing of the old primary\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading from standbys without considering lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failover never tested\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"wal-and-streaming","text":"WAL and streaming"},{"id":"asynchronous-or-synchronous","text":"Asynchronous or synchronous"},{"id":"lag","text":"Lag"},{"id":"automatic-failover","text":"Automatic failover"},{"id":"split-brain-and-fencing","text":"Split brain and fencing"},{"id":"incident-a-primary-that-filled-its-disk-with-retained-logs","text":"Incident: A primary that filled its disk with retained logs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL high availability","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/high-availability.html"},{"t":"Log shipping and standby servers","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/warm-standby.html"},{"t":"Patroni","u":"https://github.com/patroni/patroni"}],"text":"A single database server is a single point of failure. Replication keeps copies, and a failover process promotes one when the primary dies. The hard part is doing it without losing data or ending up with two primaries. What you will learn Write ahead log and streaming replication Asynchronous versus synchronous replication Replication lag and what it means for reads Automatic failover with Patroni and a consensus store Split brain, fencing and promotion safety WAL and streaming PostgreSQL writes every change to the write ahead log first. A standby connects to the primary and replays that log continuously. The standby is a read only copy that can serve queries and take over. Replication slots keep the log until each standby has received it, but a stuck standby can fill the primary disk with retained log. Asynchronous or synchronous In asynchronous replication the primary commits without waiting, so it is fast but a crash can lose the last few transactions. Synchronous replication waits for at least one standby, which avoids loss but adds latency and can block writes if the standby is gone. Quorum settings such as ANY 1 (s1, s2) give a compromise. Lag Replication lag is the delay between the primary and a standby. Measure it in bytes and in seconds. Reads from a lagging standby can return old data, so route read your writes queries to the primary or wait for a log position. Alert on lag before it becomes minutes. Automatic failover Patroni runs next to each PostgreSQL instance and keeps a leader key in a consensus store such as etcd. If the leader key expires, the healthiest standby is promoted and the others follow it. Applications connect through a proxy or a virtual address that points to the current leader. Split brain and fencing Split brain means two nodes both accept writes. Prevent it with a consensus store, a watchdog that restarts a node that cannot reach it and a proxy that checks the role. After a failover, the old primary must not rejoin as primary: it needs to be rewound or rebuilt from the new one. PostgreSQL replication and high availability Flow in order: 1, Application to Primary; 2, Primary to Standby; 3, Standby to Consensus store. Outside party Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Outside party What it does: Writes and reads. If it fails or is weak: Hard coded host. Primary Data store What it does: Accepts writes. If it fails or is weak: Crash. Standby Data store What it does: Replays WAL. If it fails or is weak: Lag. Consensus store Step What it does: Holds the leader key. If it fails or is weak: Quorum lost. Incident: A primary that filled its disk with retained logs Incident A forgotten replica made the primary retain WAL until the disk was full. $ psql -c \"select slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) as retained from pg_replication_slots\" slot_name | active | retained old_rep_2 | f | 412 GB (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A standby decommissioned weeks earlier still had a replication slot, so the primary kept every log segment since then. Dropping the slot freed the space, and the team added an alert on retained WAL per slot and a step in the decommission checklist. Common mistakes Forgotten replication slots Synchronous replication with a single standby No fencing of the old primary Reading from standbys without considering lag Failover never tested"},"db-ops/backups-pitr-and-restore-testing":{"html":"\u003cp>Replication is not a backup: it copies mistakes and deletions instantly. Backups with point in time recovery let you return to the moment before a bad change, if you have tested them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logical versus physical backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Base backup plus WAL archiving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Point in time recovery in practice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tools: pgBackRest and cloud snapshots\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restore drills and monitoring\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logical-and-physical\">Logical and physical\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A logical backup with \u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code> exports data as SQL and is portable across versions but slow to restore on large databases. A physical backup copies the data files and is fast to restore. Large production databases rely on physical backups, with dumps for specific tables or version moves.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"base-plus-wal\">Base plus WAL\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A base backup is a full copy of the data directory. Continuous archiving then copies each WAL segment to storage as it fills. To restore, you take the base backup and replay the archived WAL up to a chosen time. The archive must be complete, so a missing segment breaks recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pitr\">PITR\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Point in time recovery lets you set a target such as \u003ccode>recovery_target_time = 2026-11-10 09:14:00\u003c/code>. After an accidental delete at 09:15 you restore to 09:14 into a separate server, copy out the lost rows and keep production running. Practise the steps so they are not new during an incident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tools\">Tools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>pgBackRest and similar tools handle full, differential and incremental backups, compression, encryption, parallel restore and retention. Managed services provide snapshots and PITR, but you still choose retention, test restores and protect backups from deletion by the same account that runs the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drills-and-monitoring\">Drills and monitoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Restore a backup into a scratch environment on a schedule and run integrity checks and a sample query. Record how long it took and compare it with the recovery time objective. Alert on archive failures, the age of the last successful backup and the size of the WAL archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"db&gt;arch;db&gt;base;base&gt;drill\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Backups, point in time recovery and restore testing\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Primary\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"arch\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">WAL archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"base\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Base backups\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drill\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Restore drill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Primary to WAL archive; 2, Primary to Base backups; 3, Base backups to Restore drill.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Primary \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes WAL.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Archive fails.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"arch\">\u003cdt>WAL archive \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Continuous copy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Gap in segments.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"base\">\u003cdt>Base backups \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Full copies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old or missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drill\">\u003cdt>Restore drill \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Proves it works.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-restore-that-stopped-at-a-missing-segment\">Incident: A restore that stopped at a missing segment\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a recovery the database refused to continue because one WAL segment was missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pgbackrest --stanza=main check\npgbackrest --stanza=main info | grep -A2 &quot;wal archive min/max&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pgbackrest --stanza=main check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR: [082]: WAL segment 0000000100000A3B000000F2 was not archived before the 60000ms timeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pgbackrest --stanza=main info | grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wal archive min/max&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        wal archive min/max (15): 0000000100000A3B000000A1/0000000100000A3B000000F1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The archive command had been failing for two days because a credential expired and nobody was alerted. The team renewed the credential, took a fresh full backup to cover the gap and added an alert on archive errors and on the age of the newest archived segment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating replication as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups stored in the same account and region\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never restoring them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on archive failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention that is too short for late discovered mistakes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"logical-and-physical","text":"Logical and physical"},{"id":"base-plus-wal","text":"Base plus WAL"},{"id":"pitr","text":"PITR"},{"id":"tools","text":"Tools"},{"id":"drills-and-monitoring","text":"Drills and monitoring"},{"id":"incident-a-restore-that-stopped-at-a-missing-segment","text":"Incident: A restore that stopped at a missing segment"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous archiving and PITR","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html"},{"t":"pgBackRest","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/"},{"t":"Log shipping and standby servers","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/warm-standby.html"}],"text":"Replication is not a backup: it copies mistakes and deletions instantly. Backups with point in time recovery let you return to the moment before a bad change, if you have tested them. What you will learn Logical versus physical backups Base backup plus WAL archiving Point in time recovery in practice Tools: pgBackRest and cloud snapshots Restore drills and monitoring Logical and physical A logical backup with pg_dump exports data as SQL and is portable across versions but slow to restore on large databases. A physical backup copies the data files and is fast to restore. Large production databases rely on physical backups, with dumps for specific tables or version moves. Base plus WAL A base backup is a full copy of the data directory. Continuous archiving then copies each WAL segment to storage as it fills. To restore, you take the base backup and replay the archived WAL up to a chosen time. The archive must be complete, so a missing segment breaks recovery. PITR Point in time recovery lets you set a target such as recovery_target_time = 2026-11-10 09:14:00 . After an accidental delete at 09:15 you restore to 09:14 into a separate server, copy out the lost rows and keep production running. Practise the steps so they are not new during an incident. Tools pgBackRest and similar tools handle full, differential and incremental backups, compression, encryption, parallel restore and retention. Managed services provide snapshots and PITR, but you still choose retention, test restores and protect backups from deletion by the same account that runs the database. Drills and monitoring Restore a backup into a scratch environment on a schedule and run integrity checks and a sample query. Record how long it took and compare it with the recovery time objective. Alert on archive failures, the age of the last successful backup and the size of the WAL archive. Backups, point in time recovery and restore testing Flow in order: 1, Primary to WAL archive; 2, Primary to Base backups; 3, Base backups to Restore drill. Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Primary Data store What it does: Writes WAL. If it fails or is weak: Archive fails. WAL archive Data store What it does: Continuous copy. If it fails or is weak: Gap in segments. Base backups Data store What it does: Full copies. If it fails or is weak: Old or missing. Restore drill Observability What it does: Proves it works. If it fails or is weak: Never tested. Incident: A restore that stopped at a missing segment Incident During a recovery the database refused to continue because one WAL segment was missing. $ pgbackrest --stanza=main check ERROR: [082]: WAL segment 0000000100000A3B000000F2 was not archived before the 60000ms timeout $ pgbackrest --stanza=main info | grep -A2 \"wal archive min/max\" wal archive min/max (15): 0000000100000A3B000000A1/0000000100000A3B000000F1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The archive command had been failing for two days because a credential expired and nobody was alerted. The team renewed the credential, took a fresh full backup to cover the gap and added an alert on archive errors and on the age of the newest archived segment. Common mistakes Treating replication as a backup Backups stored in the same account and region Never restoring them No alert on archive failures Retention that is too short for late discovered mistakes"},"db-ops/connection-pooling-and-capacity":{"html":"\u003cp>A database does not slow down gradually when connections grow: it hits a wall. Pooling and capacity limits keep you on the right side of it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why connections are expensive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pool modes: session, transaction and statement\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sizing the pool and max_connections\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory: shared buffers, work_mem and per connection costs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Saturation signals\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-of-connections\">Cost of connections\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In PostgreSQL each connection is a process that uses memory and competes for CPU. Thousands of idle connections waste memory, and many active ones cause context switching. Applications that open a connection per request or per pod multiply the problem, especially during scale out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pool-modes\">Pool modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>PgBouncer sits between applications and the database. In session mode a client keeps a server connection until it disconnects. In transaction mode it gets one only for the duration of a transaction, which allows thousands of clients to share a few dozen server connections. Transaction mode breaks features that need session state, such as session level advisory locks, \u003ccode>SET\u003c/code> and unnamed prepared statements without care.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sizing\">Sizing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The number of useful active connections is roughly a small multiple of the CPU cores, often between two and four times. More just queues. Set the pool size to that number, leave room for administrators and replication, and set \u003ccode>max_connections\u003c/code> above the total of all pools plus a margin.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory\">Memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>shared_buffers\u003c/code> is often set to about a quarter of RAM. \u003ccode>work_mem\u003c/code> is allocated per sort or hash operation per connection, so a large value with many connections can exhaust memory. Raise it for specific queries or roles, not globally, and watch for the operating system killing the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"saturation-signals\">Saturation signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Watch active versus idle connections, connections waiting in the pool, query queue time, lock waits and CPU. The first sign is rising wait time in the pool while the database CPU is moderate. Alerts should fire on waiting clients, not only on errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pods&gt;pool;pool&gt;db;db&gt;mon\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Connection pooling and capacity\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pods\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pool\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PgBouncer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PostgreSQL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pool metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application pods to PgBouncer; 2, PgBouncer to PostgreSQL; 3, PostgreSQL to Pool metrics.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pods\">\u003cdt>Application pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many clients.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pool per pod.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pool\">\u003cdt>PgBouncer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shares connections.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong mode.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>PostgreSQL \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limited backends.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Memory exhaustion.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Pool metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Waiting clients.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not watched.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-too-many-connections-after-a-scale-out\">Incident: Too many connections after a scale out\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Doubling the application pods made the database refuse new connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select state, count(*) from pg_stat_activity group by state&quot;\npsql -c &quot;show max_connections&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select state, count(*) from pg_stat_activity group by state&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> state  | count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> idle   | 870\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> active | 24\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;show max_connections&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> max_connections\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each pod kept ten idle connections and the new pods pushed the total to the limit while only 24 were doing work. PgBouncer in transaction mode with a pool of 40 connections replaced the per pod pools, and the application settings were changed to open fewer connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Raising max_connections instead of pooling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Transaction pooling with session state features\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Large global work_mem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on waiting clients\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A pool per pod with no total limit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cost-of-connections","text":"Cost of connections"},{"id":"pool-modes","text":"Pool modes"},{"id":"sizing","text":"Sizing"},{"id":"memory","text":"Memory"},{"id":"saturation-signals","text":"Saturation signals"},{"id":"incident-too-many-connections-after-a-scale-out","text":"Incident: Too many connections after a scale out"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PgBouncer","u":"https://www.pgbouncer.org/"},{"t":"Connection settings","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-connection.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL statistics views","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"}],"text":"A database does not slow down gradually when connections grow: it hits a wall. Pooling and capacity limits keep you on the right side of it. What you will learn Why connections are expensive Pool modes: session, transaction and statement Sizing the pool and max_connections Memory: shared buffers, work_mem and per connection costs Saturation signals Cost of connections In PostgreSQL each connection is a process that uses memory and competes for CPU. Thousands of idle connections waste memory, and many active ones cause context switching. Applications that open a connection per request or per pod multiply the problem, especially during scale out. Pool modes PgBouncer sits between applications and the database. In session mode a client keeps a server connection until it disconnects. In transaction mode it gets one only for the duration of a transaction, which allows thousands of clients to share a few dozen server connections. Transaction mode breaks features that need session state, such as session level advisory locks, SET and unnamed prepared statements without care. Sizing The number of useful active connections is roughly a small multiple of the CPU cores, often between two and four times. More just queues. Set the pool size to that number, leave room for administrators and replication, and set max_connections above the total of all pools plus a margin. Memory shared_buffers is often set to about a quarter of RAM. work_mem is allocated per sort or hash operation per connection, so a large value with many connections can exhaust memory. Raise it for specific queries or roles, not globally, and watch for the operating system killing the database. Saturation signals Watch active versus idle connections, connections waiting in the pool, query queue time, lock waits and CPU. The first sign is rising wait time in the pool while the database CPU is moderate. Alerts should fire on waiting clients, not only on errors. Connection pooling and capacity Flow in order: 1, Application pods to PgBouncer; 2, PgBouncer to PostgreSQL; 3, PostgreSQL to Pool metrics. Outside party Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application pods Outside party What it does: Many clients. If it fails or is weak: Pool per pod. PgBouncer Step What it does: Shares connections. If it fails or is weak: Wrong mode. PostgreSQL Data store What it does: Limited backends. If it fails or is weak: Memory exhaustion. Pool metrics Observability What it does: Waiting clients. If it fails or is weak: Not watched. Incident: Too many connections after a scale out Incident Doubling the application pods made the database refuse new connections. $ psql -c \"select state, count(*) from pg_stat_activity group by state\" state | count idle | 870 active | 24 $ psql -c \"show max_connections\" max_connections 1000 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Each pod kept ten idle connections and the new pods pushed the total to the limit while only 24 were doing work. PgBouncer in transaction mode with a pool of 40 connections replaced the per pod pools, and the application settings were changed to open fewer connections. Common mistakes Raising max_connections instead of pooling Transaction pooling with session state features Large global work_mem No alert on waiting clients A pool per pod with no total limit"},"db-ops/zero-downtime-migrations-and-upgrades":{"html":"\u003cp>A schema change that takes a lock on a busy table is an outage. Safe migrations split a change into small compatible steps, and major upgrades need a rehearsed plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Locks taken by common DDL\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expand and contract\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe patterns: indexes, columns and constraints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Major version upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rollback planning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locks\">Locks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Many \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> operations take an exclusive lock, and a long running query ahead of them in the queue blocks everything behind. Set \u003ccode>lock_timeout\u003c/code> so a migration fails fast instead of piling up waiters, and run it when the table is quiet. Know whether each statement rewrites the table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expand-and-contract\">Expand and contract\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>First expand: add the new column or table, nullable, without removing anything. Deploy code that writes both and reads the old. Backfill in small batches. Then switch reads to the new structure. Finally contract: remove the old column once nothing uses it. Each step is safe to roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-patterns\">Safe patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create indexes with \u003ccode>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>, which avoids blocking writes but takes longer and can leave an invalid index if it fails. Add a column with a default in recent versions without a rewrite. Add a foreign key or check constraint as \u003ccode>NOT VALID\u003c/code> and validate it afterwards, which needs a weaker lock.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"major-upgrades\">Major upgrades\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>pg_upgrade\u003c/code> upgrades in place quickly, especially with link mode, but needs a window and a tested rollback. Logical replication builds a new version cluster in parallel and lets you switch with seconds of downtime. After the upgrade, run \u003ccode>ANALYZE\u003c/code> to rebuild statistics and watch query plans.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollback\">Rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before a risky step write the rollback. For schema changes it is often the reverse step. For upgrades keep the old cluster intact until the new one has been in use long enough. Rehearse on a recent copy of production data and measure the timing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"exp&gt;mig;mig&gt;con;con&gt;up\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Zero downtime migrations and upgrades\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"exp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Expand\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mig\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Migrate code and data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"con\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Contract\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"up\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Major upgrade\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Expand to Migrate code and data; 2, Migrate code and data to Contract; 3, Contract to Major upgrade.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"exp\">\u003cdt>Expand \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add compatible structure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blocking lock.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mig\">\u003cdt>Migrate code and data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Dual write, backfill.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Big batch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"con\">\u003cdt>Contract \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Remove old structure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Still used.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"up\">\u003cdt>Major upgrade \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Parallel cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-index-build-that-blocked-writes-for-ten-minutes\">Incident: An index build that blocked writes for ten minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A plain index creation on a busy table held a lock and orders could not be saved.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select pid, wait_event_type, query from pg_stat_activity where wait_event_type = 'Lock' limit 2&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select pid, wait_event_type, query from pg_stat_activity where wait_event_type = &#x27;Lock&#x27; limit 2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | wait_event_type | query\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 4411 | Lock            | insert into orders (...)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 4412 | Lock            | insert into orders (...)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team created the index without CONCURRENTLY, which blocks writes for the whole build. The next time they used CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY with a lock timeout, checked for an invalid index afterwards and scheduled it outside the peak.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Migrations with no lock timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping a column before code stops using it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating indexes without CONCURRENTLY on busy tables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading with no rehearsal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting ANALYZE after an upgrade\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"locks","text":"Locks"},{"id":"expand-and-contract","text":"Expand and contract"},{"id":"safe-patterns","text":"Safe patterns"},{"id":"major-upgrades","text":"Major upgrades"},{"id":"rollback","text":"Rollback"},{"id":"incident-an-index-build-that-blocked-writes-for-ten-minutes","text":"Incident: An index build that blocked writes for ten minutes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"},{"t":"pg_upgrade","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgupgrade.html"},{"t":"Logical replication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/logical-replication.html"}],"text":"A schema change that takes a lock on a busy table is an outage. Safe migrations split a change into small compatible steps, and major upgrades need a rehearsed plan. What you will learn Locks taken by common DDL Expand and contract Safe patterns: indexes, columns and constraints Major version upgrades Rollback planning Locks Many ALTER TABLE operations take an exclusive lock, and a long running query ahead of them in the queue blocks everything behind. Set lock_timeout so a migration fails fast instead of piling up waiters, and run it when the table is quiet. Know whether each statement rewrites the table. Expand and contract First expand: add the new column or table, nullable, without removing anything. Deploy code that writes both and reads the old. Backfill in small batches. Then switch reads to the new structure. Finally contract: remove the old column once nothing uses it. Each step is safe to roll back. Safe patterns Create indexes with CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY , which avoids blocking writes but takes longer and can leave an invalid index if it fails. Add a column with a default in recent versions without a rewrite. Add a foreign key or check constraint as NOT VALID and validate it afterwards, which needs a weaker lock. Major upgrades pg_upgrade upgrades in place quickly, especially with link mode, but needs a window and a tested rollback. Logical replication builds a new version cluster in parallel and lets you switch with seconds of downtime. After the upgrade, run ANALYZE to rebuild statistics and watch query plans. Rollback Before a risky step write the rollback. For schema changes it is often the reverse step. For upgrades keep the old cluster intact until the new one has been in use long enough. Rehearse on a recent copy of production data and measure the timing. Zero downtime migrations and upgrades Flow in order: 1, Expand to Migrate code and data; 2, Migrate code and data to Contract; 3, Contract to Major upgrade. Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Expand Step What it does: Add compatible structure. If it fails or is weak: Blocking lock. Migrate code and data Step What it does: Dual write, backfill. If it fails or is weak: Big batch. Contract Step What it does: Remove old structure. If it fails or is weak: Still used. Major upgrade Service What it does: Parallel cluster. If it fails or is weak: No rollback. Incident: An index build that blocked writes for ten minutes Incident A plain index creation on a busy table held a lock and orders could not be saved. $ psql -c \"select pid, wait_event_type, query from pg_stat_activity where wait_event_type = &#x27;Lock&#x27; limit 2\" pid | wait_event_type | query 4411 | Lock | insert into orders (...) 4412 | Lock | insert into orders (...) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The team created the index without CONCURRENTLY, which blocks writes for the whole build. The next time they used CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY with a lock timeout, checked for an invalid index afterwards and scheduled it outside the peak. Common mistakes Migrations with no lock timeout Dropping a column before code stops using it Creating indexes without CONCURRENTLY on busy tables Upgrading with no rehearsal Forgetting ANALYZE after an upgrade"},"db-ops/redis-and-mongodb-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>Not every system uses PostgreSQL. Redis and MongoDB appear in most estates, and each has its own durability and capacity rules that operators must know.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Redis persistence: RDB and AOF\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Redis memory, eviction and big keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Redis replication, Sentinel and Cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>MongoDB replica sets and write concern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>MongoDB indexes, sharding and backups\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"redis-persistence\">Redis persistence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Redis keeps data in memory. RDB snapshots write the dataset at intervals and are compact but can lose recent writes. The append only file logs each write and can fsync every second, which limits loss to about a second. Many caches use no persistence, and the team must know which data can be lost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-eviction\">Memory and eviction\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>maxmemory\u003c/code> below the machine memory with room for fork and copy on write during snapshots. The eviction policy decides what happens when it is full: \u003ccode>allkeys-lru\u003c/code> suits caches, while \u003ccode>noeviction\u003c/code> makes writes fail, which is right for data you must not lose. Find big keys and unbounded lists with \u003ccode>redis-cli --bigkeys\u003c/code> or memory analysis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"redis-availability\">Redis availability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A primary with replicas can be managed by Sentinel, which monitors and promotes a replica, or by Redis Cluster, which shards keys over many primaries by hash slot. Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can lose recent writes. Clients must handle redirections and reconnects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mongodb-replica-sets\">MongoDB replica sets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A replica set has a primary and secondaries, and elects a new primary when it fails with a majority vote, so use three or more voting members. Write concern \u003ccode>majority\u003c/code> waits until most members have the write, which protects it from rollback. Read preference controls whether reads may go to secondaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"indexes-sharding-and-backups\">Indexes, sharding and backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Queries without an index scan the whole collection. Check slow queries with the profiler and \u003ccode>explain\u003c/code>, and keep indexes few and useful. Sharding splits a collection across shards by a shard key. A poor key causes hot spots, so choose one with high cardinality and even distribution. Back up with snapshots or \u003ccode>mongodump\u003c/code> consistent with the oplog, and test restores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;redis;app&gt;mongo;mongo&gt;bk\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Redis and MongoDB operations\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Applications\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"redis\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Redis\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mongo\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">MongoDB replica set\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bk\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backups and monitoring\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Applications to Redis; 2, Applications to MongoDB replica set; 3, MongoDB replica set to Backups and monitoring.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Applications \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Read and write.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No reconnect logic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"redis\">\u003cdt>Redis \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Memory store.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Eviction or loss.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mongo\">\u003cdt>MongoDB replica set \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Primary and secondaries.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bk\">\u003cdt>Backups and monitoring \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Snapshots and alerts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Untested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-cache-that-evicted-the-sessions\">Incident: A cache that evicted the sessions\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Users were logged out when Redis reached its memory limit and removed session keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"redis-cli info memory | grep -E &quot;used_memory_human|maxmemory_human|maxmemory_policy&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>redis-cli info memory | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;used_memory_human|maxmemory_human|maxmemory_policy&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">used_memory_human:7.98G\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">maxmemory_human:8.00G\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">maxmemory_policy:allkeys-lru\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Sessions and cache entries shared one instance with an eviction policy meant for caches, so sessions were removed under pressure. The team separated sessions into their own instance with noeviction and alerts at eighty percent memory, and left the cache with allkeys-lru.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mixing caches and primary data in one Redis\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No maxmemory with snapshots enabled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Two member MongoDB replica sets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A shard key with low cardinality\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No tested restore\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"redis-persistence","text":"Redis persistence"},{"id":"memory-and-eviction","text":"Memory and eviction"},{"id":"redis-availability","text":"Redis availability"},{"id":"mongodb-replica-sets","text":"MongoDB replica sets"},{"id":"indexes-sharding-and-backups","text":"Indexes, sharding and backups"},{"id":"incident-a-cache-that-evicted-the-sessions","text":"Incident: A cache that evicted the sessions"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Redis persistence","u":"https://redis.io/docs/latest/operate/oss_and_stack/management/persistence/"},{"t":"Redis key eviction","u":"https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/eviction/"},{"t":"MongoDB replication","u":"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/replication/"}],"text":"Not every system uses PostgreSQL. Redis and MongoDB appear in most estates, and each has its own durability and capacity rules that operators must know. What you will learn Redis persistence: RDB and AOF Redis memory, eviction and big keys Redis replication, Sentinel and Cluster MongoDB replica sets and write concern MongoDB indexes, sharding and backups Redis persistence Redis keeps data in memory. RDB snapshots write the dataset at intervals and are compact but can lose recent writes. The append only file logs each write and can fsync every second, which limits loss to about a second. Many caches use no persistence, and the team must know which data can be lost. Memory and eviction Set maxmemory below the machine memory with room for fork and copy on write during snapshots. The eviction policy decides what happens when it is full: allkeys-lru suits caches, while noeviction makes writes fail, which is right for data you must not lose. Find big keys and unbounded lists with redis-cli --bigkeys or memory analysis. Redis availability A primary with replicas can be managed by Sentinel, which monitors and promotes a replica, or by Redis Cluster, which shards keys over many primaries by hash slot. Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can lose recent writes. Clients must handle redirections and reconnects. MongoDB replica sets A replica set has a primary and secondaries, and elects a new primary when it fails with a majority vote, so use three or more voting members. Write concern majority waits until most members have the write, which protects it from rollback. Read preference controls whether reads may go to secondaries. Indexes, sharding and backups Queries without an index scan the whole collection. Check slow queries with the profiler and explain , and keep indexes few and useful. Sharding splits a collection across shards by a shard key. A poor key causes hot spots, so choose one with high cardinality and even distribution. Back up with snapshots or mongodump consistent with the oplog, and test restores. Redis and MongoDB operations Flow in order: 1, Applications to Redis; 2, Applications to MongoDB replica set; 3, MongoDB replica set to Backups and monitoring. Outside party Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Applications Outside party What it does: Read and write. If it fails or is weak: No reconnect logic. Redis Data store What it does: Memory store. If it fails or is weak: Eviction or loss. MongoDB replica set Data store What it does: Primary and secondaries. If it fails or is weak: Rollback. Backups and monitoring Observability What it does: Snapshots and alerts. If it fails or is weak: Untested. Incident: A cache that evicted the sessions Incident Users were logged out when Redis reached its memory limit and removed session keys. $ redis-cli info memory | grep -E \"used_memory_human|maxmemory_human|maxmemory_policy\" used_memory_human:7.98G maxmemory_human:8.00G maxmemory_policy:allkeys-lru (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Sessions and cache entries shared one instance with an eviction policy meant for caches, so sessions were removed under pressure. The team separated sessions into their own instance with noeviction and alerts at eighty percent memory, and left the cache with allkeys-lru. Common mistakes Mixing caches and primary data in one Redis No maxmemory with snapshots enabled Two member MongoDB replica sets A shard key with low cardinality No tested restore"},"db-ops/database-incidents-and-runbooks":{"html":"\u003cp>Database incidents follow patterns: too many connections, a runaway query, locks, full disk, replication broken. A short method and good runbooks make them routine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A first five minute checklist\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnosing load: queries, locks and waits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disk, WAL and replication emergencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe actions and what not to do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing and testing runbooks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"first-five-minutes\">First five minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Confirm the impact, check whether the database is up and accepting connections, check disk and CPU, then look at connections and the longest running queries. Announce the incident and pick a commander. Avoid changing several things at once, because you will not know what worked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"queries-locks-and-waits\">Queries, locks and waits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In PostgreSQL \u003ccode>pg_stat_activity\u003c/code> shows running queries and their wait events, and \u003ccode>pg_locks\u003c/code> shows who blocks whom. A single long transaction can hold locks and prevent cleanup, which causes bloat. In MongoDB \u003ccode>currentOp\u003c/code> and the profiler play the same role. Find the blocker, not only the victims.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disk-and-replication\">Disk and replication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A full disk can stop a database. Find what is large: tables, temporary files, WAL or logs. Do not delete WAL files by hand. Free space by removing safe files, dropping unused slots or extending the volume. If a replica breaks, rebuild it from a base backup rather than guessing at repairs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-actions\">Safe actions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cancel a query with \u003ccode>pg_cancel_backend\u003c/code> before terminating it with \u003ccode>pg_terminate_backend\u003c/code>. Kill the blocker only when you understand the effect. Do not restart a database during recovery unless you must, and never run a destructive command on the only copy of the data without a backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runbooks\">Runbooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good runbook starts with the symptom, lists the checks in order with exact commands, states what is safe, has a decision for each result and names who to call. Test it by letting someone who did not write it follow it in a drill, then fix what was unclear.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;diag;diag&gt;act;act&gt;rb\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Database incidents and runbooks\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"diag\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Diagnose\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Safe action\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rb\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runbook update\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Symptom to Diagnose; 2, Diagnose to Safe action; 3, Safe action to Runbook update.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Alert or report.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Misread.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"diag\">\u003cdt>Diagnose \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queries, locks, disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing many things.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Safe action \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cancel, free space.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Destructive step.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rb\">\u003cdt>Runbook update \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Captures lessons.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never updated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-idle-transaction-that-blocked-everything\">Incident: One idle transaction that blocked everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A forgotten transaction held a lock for six hours and queries stacked up behind it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select pid, now()-xact_start as age, state, left(query,40) from pg_stat_activity where state = 'idle in transaction' order by age desc limit 2&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select pid, now()-xact_start as age, state, left(query,40) from pg_stat_activity where state = &#x27;idle in transaction&#x27; order by age desc limit 2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | age      | state               | left\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 3902 | 06:11:02 | idle in transaction | update accounts set ...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A script opened a transaction and then waited for input. The commander cancelled it after checking it was safe, queries drained within a minute and an idle in transaction timeout was set, with an alert on the age of the oldest transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing many things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting WAL files by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Killing sessions without understanding them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runbooks that nobody has tried\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout for idle in transaction sessions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"first-five-minutes","text":"First five minutes"},{"id":"queries-locks-and-waits","text":"Queries, locks and waits"},{"id":"disk-and-replication","text":"Disk and replication"},{"id":"safe-actions","text":"Safe actions"},{"id":"runbooks","text":"Runbooks"},{"id":"incident-one-idle-transaction-that-blocked-everything","text":"Incident: One idle transaction that blocked everything"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL statistics views","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"},{"t":"Explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL high availability","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/high-availability.html"}],"text":"Database incidents follow patterns: too many connections, a runaway query, locks, full disk, replication broken. A short method and good runbooks make them routine. What you will learn A first five minute checklist Diagnosing load: queries, locks and waits Disk, WAL and replication emergencies Safe actions and what not to do Writing and testing runbooks First five minutes Confirm the impact, check whether the database is up and accepting connections, check disk and CPU, then look at connections and the longest running queries. Announce the incident and pick a commander. Avoid changing several things at once, because you will not know what worked. Queries, locks and waits In PostgreSQL pg_stat_activity shows running queries and their wait events, and pg_locks shows who blocks whom. A single long transaction can hold locks and prevent cleanup, which causes bloat. In MongoDB currentOp and the profiler play the same role. Find the blocker, not only the victims. Disk and replication A full disk can stop a database. Find what is large: tables, temporary files, WAL or logs. Do not delete WAL files by hand. Free space by removing safe files, dropping unused slots or extending the volume. If a replica breaks, rebuild it from a base backup rather than guessing at repairs. Safe actions Cancel a query with pg_cancel_backend before terminating it with pg_terminate_backend . Kill the blocker only when you understand the effect. Do not restart a database during recovery unless you must, and never run a destructive command on the only copy of the data without a backup. Runbooks A good runbook starts with the symptom, lists the checks in order with exact commands, states what is safe, has a decision for each result and names who to call. Test it by letting someone who did not write it follow it in a drill, then fix what was unclear. Database incidents and runbooks Flow in order: 1, Symptom to Diagnose; 2, Diagnose to Safe action; 3, Safe action to Runbook update. Outside party Observability Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Symptom Outside party What it does: Alert or report. If it fails or is weak: Misread. Diagnose Observability What it does: Queries, locks, disk. If it fails or is weak: Changing many things. Safe action Step What it does: Cancel, free space. If it fails or is weak: Destructive step. Runbook update Data store What it does: Captures lessons. If it fails or is weak: Never updated. Incident: One idle transaction that blocked everything Incident A forgotten transaction held a lock for six hours and queries stacked up behind it. $ psql -c \"select pid, now()-xact_start as age, state, left(query,40) from pg_stat_activity where state = &#x27;idle in transaction&#x27; order by age desc limit 2\" pid | age | state | left 3902 | 06:11:02 | idle in transaction | update accounts set ... (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A script opened a transaction and then waited for input. The commander cancelled it after checking it was safe, queries drained within a minute and an idle in transaction timeout was set, with an alert on the age of the oldest transaction. Common mistakes Changing many things at once Deleting WAL files by hand Killing sessions without understanding them Runbooks that nobody has tried No timeout for idle in transaction sessions"}});